WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.156
This is Play the Recorder from Zero, by FlautaQuest. The soprano recorder, one step at a time.

00:00:07.956 --> 00:00:12.413
Today: breath and tongue and your first notes: B, A, G.

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.992
Breath and tongue. Making a good sound.

00:00:17.792 --> 00:00:27.828
The sound of the recorder depends almost entirely on your air. Your fingers choose the note; your breath and your tongue decide whether it sounds clear, sweet and steady.

00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:30.337
Into the mouth.

00:00:31.137 --> 00:00:41.321
Place the tip of the mouthpiece between your lips, just in front of your teeth. Close your lips gently around it. Do not bite it, and do not put it far into your mouth.

00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:44.337
Gentle, warm air.

00:00:45.137 --> 00:00:57.535
Blow softly, as if you were breathing on cold hands to warm them, or saying “hoo” quietly. The recorder needs very little air. If you blow hard, the sound gets shrill and the note can jump up or squeak.

00:00:58.335 --> 00:01:06.820
Keep the air steady. A good test: play one note and hold it for as long as you comfortably can, keeping the sound the same from start to end.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.006
Starting notes with the tongue.

00:01:10.806 --> 00:01:26.839
Each note should begin with a light touch of the tongue, as if you were saying ”du”. The tongue touches the ridge just behind your top teeth and lets the air go. This is called tonguing. It gives every note a clean start, and it separates repeated notes so you can hear each one.

00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:33.433
Do not start notes by pushing air with your chest (“hoo, hoo”): the notes will sound blurry and uneven.

00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:35.500
Breathing.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:50.623
Breathe in through your mouth, quickly and quietly, at the end of a phrase, just as you do when you speak or sing. Take a calm breath, not a huge one. In songs, good places to breathe are after long notes and at the end of each line.

00:01:51.423 --> 00:01:59.367
Tip: If a note squeaks, check three things in this order: are you blowing too hard, is every hole fully covered, and is the window clear?

00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:01.599
Practice.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:11.077
First: cover only the thumb hole and hole 1, the note B, and play long notes of about four seconds. Keep the sound steady and soft.

00:02:11.877 --> 00:02:17.477
Second: play the same note four times with the tongue, “du du du du”, at an even speed.

00:02:18.277 --> 00:02:21.963
Third: try “du du du du” faster, and then very slowly.

00:02:22.763 --> 00:02:28.738
Fourth: play one long note, breathe, and play another. Make the breath quick and quiet.

00:02:30.538 --> 00:02:35.790
Your first notes: B, A, G. Three notes, many songs.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:47.898
The first three notes almost everyone learns on the recorder are B, A and G. They use only the left hand, and they sound good right away. With them you can already play real tunes.

00:02:49.398 --> 00:02:50.396
The fingerings.

00:02:51.196 --> 00:02:53.581
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:02:54.381 --> 00:02:57.295
B: thumb and first finger of the left hand.

00:02:58.095 --> 00:03:00.045
A: add the second finger.

00:03:00.845 --> 00:03:02.767
G: add the third finger.

00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:10.303
Notice that each new note just adds one finger. Going from B to A to G is like walking down a ladder.

00:03:11.803 --> 00:03:13.314
Moving between notes.

00:03:14.114 --> 00:03:23.401
Lift and lower your fingers together with the tongue: change the fingering, then say “du”. Keep your fingers close to the holes, and keep the thumb covered all the time.

00:03:24.901 --> 00:03:27.038
First tune: Hot Cross Buns.

00:03:27.838 --> 00:03:41.058
This traditional tune uses only B, A and G. Each letter is one beat; a dash means hold the note for another beat. Letters written together, like GGGG, are quicker: two notes per beat.

00:03:41.858 --> 00:03:44.243
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:45.743 --> 00:03:48.373
Second tune: Au clair de la lune.

00:03:49.173 --> 00:03:53.475
The beginning of this old French song also uses only G, A and B.

00:03:54.275 --> 00:03:56.660
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:05.648
Tip: Play slowly first. When every note is clean at a slow speed, then speed up. The tune will come by itself.

00:04:07.148 --> 00:04:07.880
Practice.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.493
First: play B, A and G with long notes, two beats each, going down and then back up.

00:04:15.293 --> 00:04:20.602
Second: play B A G A B, over and over, using your tongue on every note.

00:04:21.402 --> 00:04:25.243
Third: learn Hot Cross Buns, then Au clair de la lune.

00:04:26.043 --> 00:04:30.109
Fourth: make up your own little tune with only B, A and G.

00:04:31.909 --> 00:04:33.158
That's all for today.

00:04:33.958 --> 00:04:35.349
If you want to keep going:

00:04:36.149 --> 00:04:46.969
FlautaQuest is the app behind the ebook. It teaches the soprano recorder from zero, in short, step-by-step lessons, in the style of language-learning apps like Duolingo.

00:04:47.769 --> 00:04:50.675
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to go with it:

00:04:51.475 --> 00:04:53.710
holding the recorder and breathing well.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:56.400
the fingerings of each new note.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.787
reading simple music.

00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:02.534
learning your first songs, one step at a time.

00:05:03.334 --> 00:05:08.415
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who wants to start playing the recorder.

00:05:09.215 --> 00:05:14.050
This episode of Play the Recorder from Zero was narrated with a synthetic voice.

